C Function:
```c
char* appendSlashIfValid(const char* str, size_t limit) {
    if (strlen(str) <= limit) {
        char *newStr = malloc(strlen(str) + 2);
        if (newStr == NULL) return NULL;
        strcpy(newStr, str);
        newStr[strlen(str)] = '/';
        newStr[strlen(str)+1] = '\0';
        return newStr;
    }
    return NULL;
}
```
This function takes a string and a size limit as input, checks if the input string does not exceed the size limit, appends a slash to the end of the string and returns a new allocated memory block with the modified string. If the input string exceeds the size limit, it returns NULL to signal an error.

This function assumes that the caller is responsible for freeing the allocated memory when it's no longer needed using `free(newStr)`. It also does not handle the edge case where `strlen(str) + 2` might cause buffer overflow."]

