[' To create a distributed, fault-tolerant system for real-time IoT data processing across multiple geographic locations, consider using a combination of the following technologies:

1. Message Broker: Apache Kafka or Amazon MQ (for AWS) for high-throughput, low-latency messaging and streaming data processing.

2. Stream Processing Framework: Apache Flink or Apache Beam (for stream processing across multiple regions) to handle intermittent and noisy data, perform real-time data aggregation, anomaly detection, and timely alerts.

3. Scalable Data Storage Solution: Apache Cassandra or Amazon DynamoDB (for AWS) for high performance, distributed, and fault-tolerant data storage with efficient resource utilization.

4. External Data Integration: Use REST APIs to integrate weather data from services like OpenWeatherMap or Dark Sky, as well as geospatial databases such as OpenStreetMap for contextual information enrichment.

5. Secrets Management System: HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager to manage and secure API keys and credentials.

6. Access Control: Implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) using tools like Apache Ranger, Open Policy Agent (OPA), or AWS IAM for data protection.

7. Encryption & Data Masking: Use solutions like VeraCrypt for end-to-end encryption, and data masking tools like DbProtect or PyCRYPTODome for sensitive data protection.

8. Resource Optimization: Containerize your microservices using Docker and orchestrate them with Kubernetes for efficient resource utilization and seamless deployment.

9. Monitoring & Alerting: Prometheus, Grafana, and Jaeger (for distributed tracing) for comprehensive monitoring and alerting of performance bottlenecks and anomalies.

10. Cloud Provider Selection: Evaluate various cloud provider offerings like AWS, Google Cloud Platform (GCP), or Microsoft Azure based on long-term scalability, system performance, cost, and regulatory compliance.

11. Modular Design & Best Practices: Adhere to best practices for modular design, test-driven development, CI/CD workflows, automated testing, and proactive monitoring systems.

Here is an example code snippet using Apache Flink, Kafka, and Cassandra for real-time data processing and aggregation:

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.serialization.SimpleStringSchema;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.connectors.kafka.FlinkKafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.connectors.kafka.FlinkKafkaProducer;
import org.apache.flink.table.api.bridge.java.StreamTableEnvironment;
import com.datastax.cassandra.connector.CassandraConnector;

public class FlinkIoTDataProcessor {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    final StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
    final StreamTableEnvironment tableEnv = StreamTableEnvironment.create(env);

    // Kafka consumer and producer configurations
    Properties kafkaProps = new Properties();
    kafkaProps.setProperty("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
    FlinkKafkaConsumer<String> sensorInput = new FlinkKafkaConsumer<>(
        "sensor-data",
        new SimpleStringSchema(),
        kafkaProps);

    // Cassandra connection and table creation
    CassandraConnector cassandra = new CassandraConnector();
    cassandra.connect(Cluster.builder().addContactPoint("127.0.0.1").build());
    Session session = cassandra.getSession();
    Statement stmt = new SimpleStatement("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS sensor_data (deviceId int, timestamp asof time, data double, PRIMARY KEY (deviceId, timestamp))");
    session.execute(stmt);

    // Flink table definition and SQL query for data aggregation and anomaly detection
    tableEnv.registerTableSource("sensor_input", sensorInput, "deviceId, data");
    tableEnv.createTemporaryView("sensor_data", "deviceId, timestamp asof(rowtime), data");

    tableEnv.sqlUpdate("CREATE STREAM sensor_stream (deviceId int, timestamp asof(rowtime), data double) FROM sensor_data").await();

    // Anomaly detection SQL query and sink for alerting
    String anomalyDetectionQuery = "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS anomaly_detection AS \n" +
                                   "SELECT deviceId, timestamp asof(rowtime), data, AVG(data) OVER (window 5 minutes) as avg_data, STDDEV(data) OVER (window 5 minutes) as std_deviation,\n" +
                                   "       (data - avg_data) / std_deviation as anomaly_score\n" +
                                   "FROM sensor_stream";
    tableEnv.sqlQuery(anomalyDetectionQuery).execute().addSink(new MyAlertSink()).name("Anomaly Detection").print();

    env.execute("Real-time IoT Data Processing");
  }
}
```']

